{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019iJkXBUrJLFsVWo9hy9Sfi","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# PRD→Plan Integration\n     2→\n     3→## User Story\n     4→\n     5→**As a** developer using Ralph Wiggum\n     6→**I want to** use my generated PRD documents as context for plan generation\n     7→**So that** my implementation plan is informed by the product requirements\n     8→\n     9→## Problem Statement\n    10→\n    11→After generating PRD.md and AUDIENCE_JTBD.md, users cannot easily use them as context for generating implementation plans. The Plan Generator doesn't have an option to include PRD context, and file save verification is unclear.\n    12→\n    13→## Acceptance Criteria\n    14→\n    15→### AC-1: \"Use PRD Documents\" Toggle\n    16→- [ ] Plan Generator has a checkbox to include PRD.md and AUDIENCE_JTBD.md\n    17→- [ ] When enabled, PRD content is prepended to the generation prompt\n    18→- [ ] Toggle is disabled during generation\n    19→- [ ] Clear visual indication of what the toggle does\n    20→\n    21→### AC-2: File Save Confirmation\n    22→- [ ] When files are inserted, dashboard shows confirmation message\n    23→- [ ] config:saved WebSocket message is handled\n    24→- [ ] Confirmation auto-clears after 3 seconds\n    25→- [ ] Files are saved to TARGET_PROJECT_PATH (not Ralph path)\n    26→\n    27→### AC-3: Generate Button Logic\n    28→- [ ] Generate button enabled when PRD docs selected (even without goal text)\n    29→- [ ] Work-scoped mode still requires workScope\n    30→- [ ] Button disabled during generation\n    31→\n    32→## Implementation Details\n    33→\n    34→### Files to Modify\n    35→\n    36→| File | Changes |\n    37→|------|---------|\n    38→| `dashboard/src/hooks/useWebSocket.ts` | Add config:saved handler, savedFiles state |\n    39→| `dashboard/src/components/PlanGenerator.tsx` | Add usePrdDocs checkbox, update disabled logic |\n    40→| `dashboard/src/types/index.ts` | Add usePrdContext to GeneratePlanCommand |\n    41→| `dashboard/server/index.ts` | Read PRD files when usePrdContext is true |\n    42→| `dashboard/server/planGenerator.ts` | Accept prdContext, prepend to prompt |\n    43→\n    44→### Key Code Changes\n    45→\n    46→**useWebSocket.ts - Add saved files state:**\n    47→```typescript\n    48→const [savedFiles, setSavedFiles] = useState<Set<string>>(new Set());\n    49→\n    50→case 'config:saved':\n    51→  setSavedFiles(prev => new Set([...prev, message.payload.file]));\n    52→  setTimeout(() => {\n    53→    setSavedFiles(prev => {\n    54→      const next = new Set(prev);\n    55→      next.delete(message.payload.file);\n    56→      return next;\n    57→    });\n    58→  }, 3000);\n    59→  break;\n    60→```\n    61→\n    62→**PlanGenerator.tsx - Update button disabled logic:**\n    63→```typescript\n    64→// Before:\n    65→disabled={!goal.trim() || (mode === 'plan-work' && !workScope.trim())}\n    66→\n    67→// After:\n    68→disabled={(!goal.trim() && !usePrdDocs) || (mode === 'plan-work' && !workScope.trim())}\n    69→```\n    70→\n    71→## Required Tests\n    72→\n    73→1. **PRD Context Toggle Test**\n    74→   - Generate plan with PRD toggle off → prompt doesn't include PRD content\n    75→   - Generate plan with PRD toggle on → prompt includes PRD.md and AUDIENCE_JTBD.md content\n    76→\n    77→2. **File Save Verification Test**\n    78→   - Insert PRD files → verify files exist in TARGET_PROJECT_PATH\n    79→   - Verify confirmation message appears\n    80→   - Verify message clears after 3 seconds\n    81→\n    82→3. **Button State Test**\n    83→   - No goal, no PRD toggle → button disabled\n    84→   - No goal, PRD toggle on → button enabled\n    85→   - Goal entered, PRD toggle off → button enabled\n    86→   - Work-scoped mode without workScope → button disabled\n    87→\n    88→## Dependencies\n    89→\n    90→- Requires PRD.md and AUDIENCE_JTBD.md to exist in project (generate first)\n    91→- Requires projectConfig.readFile() method (already exists)\n    92→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"427b9edd-afbb-48fa-af22-bb40ea7675f8","uuid":"c7631098-5b1a-49de-93a2-82a6b67c0896","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\xsams\\RalphWiggumV3\\specs\\01-prd-plan-integration.md","content":"# PRD→Plan Integration\n\n## User Story\n\n**As a** developer using Ralph Wiggum\n**I want to** use my generated PRD documents as context for plan generation\n**So that** my implementation plan is informed by the product requirements\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nAfter generating PRD.md and AUDIENCE_JTBD.md, users cannot easily use them as context for generating implementation plans. The Plan Generator doesn't have an option to include PRD context, and file save verification is unclear.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n### AC-1: \"Use PRD Documents\" Toggle\n- [ ] Plan Generator has a checkbox to include PRD.md and AUDIENCE_JTBD.md\n- [ ] When enabled, PRD content is prepended to the generation prompt\n- [ ] Toggle is disabled during generation\n- [ ] Clear visual indication of what the toggle does\n\n### AC-2: File Save Confirmation\n- [ ] When files are inserted, dashboard shows confirmation message\n- [ ] config:saved WebSocket message is handled\n- [ ] Confirmation auto-clears after 3 seconds\n- [ ] Files are saved to TARGET_PROJECT_PATH (not Ralph path)\n\n### AC-3: Generate Button Logic\n- [ ] Generate button enabled when PRD docs selected (even without goal text)\n- [ ] Work-scoped mode still requires workScope\n- [ ] Button disabled during generation\n\n## Implementation Details\n\n### Files to Modify\n\n| File | Changes |\n|------|---------|\n| `dashboard/src/hooks/useWebSocket.ts` | Add config:saved handler, savedFiles state |\n| `dashboard/src/components/PlanGenerator.tsx` | Add usePrdDocs checkbox, update disabled logic |\n| `dashboard/src/types/index.ts` | Add usePrdContext to GeneratePlanCommand |\n| `dashboard/server/index.ts` | Read PRD files when usePrdContext is true |\n| `dashboard/server/planGenerator.ts` | Accept prdContext, prepend to prompt |\n\n### Key Code Changes\n\n**useWebSocket.ts - Add saved files state:**\n```typescript\nconst [savedFiles, setSavedFiles] = useState<Set<string>>(new Set());\n\ncase 'config:saved':\n  setSavedFiles(prev => new Set([...prev, message.payload.file]));\n  setTimeout(() => {\n    setSavedFiles(prev => {\n      const next = new Set(prev);\n      next.delete(message.payload.file);\n      return next;\n    });\n  }, 3000);\n  break;\n```\n\n**PlanGenerator.tsx - Update button disabled logic:**\n```typescript\n// Before:\ndisabled={!goal.trim() || (mode === 'plan-work' && !workScope.trim())}\n\n// After:\ndisabled={(!goal.trim() && !usePrdDocs) || (mode === 'plan-work' && !workScope.trim())}\n```\n\n## Required Tests\n\n1. **PRD Context Toggle Test**\n   - Generate plan with PRD toggle off → prompt doesn't include PRD content\n   - Generate plan with PRD toggle on → prompt includes PRD.md and AUDIENCE_JTBD.md content\n\n2. **File Save Verification Test**\n   - Insert PRD files → verify files exist in TARGET_PROJECT_PATH\n   - Verify confirmation message appears\n   - Verify message clears after 3 seconds\n\n3. **Button State Test**\n   - No goal, no PRD toggle → button disabled\n   - No goal, PRD toggle on → button enabled\n   - Goal entered, PRD toggle off → button enabled\n   - Work-scoped mode without workScope → button disabled\n\n## Dependencies\n\n- Requires PRD.md and AUDIENCE_JTBD.md to exist in project (generate first)\n- Requires projectConfig.readFile() method (already exists)\n","numLines":92,"startLine":1,"totalLines":92}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019UN1NmaHADdwGKxP9th4TJ","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Implementation Plan Parser Enhancement\n     2→\n     3→## User Story\n     4→\n     5→**As a** developer monitoring Ralph progress\n     6→**I want** the Implementation Plan card to show accurate task counts\n     7→**So that** I can see real progress regardless of task format used\n     8→\n     9→## Problem Statement\n    10→\n    11→The Implementation Plan section shows \"0/0\" and \"No tasks found\" even when IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md exists with tasks. The parser only recognizes markdown checkbox format, but real plans use various formats including user stories (US-XXX), numbered lists, and emoji markers.\n    12→\n    13→## Acceptance Criteria\n    14→\n    15→### AC-1: Checkbox Format Support (Existing)\n    16→- [ ] Recognizes `- [ ] Task description` as incomplete\n    17→- [ ] Recognizes `- [x] Task description` as complete\n    18→- [ ] Case insensitive for X marker\n    19→\n    20→### AC-2: User Story Format Support\n    21→- [ ] Recognizes US-XXX patterns in various contexts\n    22→- [ ] Detects completion via ✅, DONE, COMPLETE markers\n    23→- [ ] Avoids duplicate counting for same US mentioned multiple times\n    24→- [ ] Extracts description after US-XXX\n    25→\n    26→### AC-3: Numbered Checkbox Support\n    27→- [ ] Recognizes `1. [ ] Task` format\n    28→- [ ] Recognizes `1. [x] Task` as complete\n    29→\n    30→### AC-4: Emoji Status Markers\n    31→- [ ] Recognizes `- ✅ Task` as complete\n    32→- [ ] Recognizes `- ⬜ Task` as incomplete\n    33→- [ ] Recognizes `- 🔲 Task` as incomplete\n    34→\n    35→### AC-5: Progress Display\n    36→- [ ] Total count reflects all recognized tasks\n    37→- [ ] Completed count reflects tasks with completion markers\n    38→- [ ] Progress bar percentage is accurate\n    39→- [ ] Updates in real-time when file changes\n    40→\n    41→## Implementation Details\n    42→\n    43→### File to Modify\n    44→\n    45→`dashboard/server/fileWatcher.ts` - parseTasks() method\n    46→\n    47→### Enhanced Parser Logic\n    48→\n    49→```typescript\n    50→private async parseTasks() {\n    51→  const content = await fs.readFile(planPath, 'utf-8');\n    52→  const tasks: Task[] = [];\n    53→  const lines = content.split('\\n');\n    54→  const seenUserStories = new Set<string>();\n    55→\n    56→  lines.forEach((line, index) => {\n    57→    // Pattern 1: Markdown checkboxes - [ ] or - [x]\n    58→    const checkboxMatch = line.match(/^-\\s*\\[([ xX])\\]\\s*(.+)$/);\n    59→    if (checkboxMatch) {\n    60→      tasks.push({\n    61→        id: `task-${index}`,\n    62→        content: checkboxMatch[2].trim(),\n    63→        completed: checkboxMatch[1].toLowerCase() === 'x',\n    64→      });\n    65→      return;\n    66→    }\n    67→\n    68→    // Pattern 2: User Stories (US-XXX format)\n    69→    const usMatch = line.match(/US-(\\d+)/);\n    70→    if (usMatch) {\n    71→      const usId = usMatch[1];\n    72→      if (seenUserStories.has(usId)) return;\n    73→      seenUserStories.add(usId);\n    74→\n    75→      const isComplete = /✅|DONE|COMPLETE|completed/i.test(line);\n    76→      let description = line\n    77→        .replace(/^[|\\s-]*/, '')\n    78→        .replace(/US-\\d+[:\\s|]*/i, '')\n    79→        .replace(/[|].*$/, '')\n    80→        .replace(/✅|⬜|🔲|DONE|TODO|COMPLETE|PENDING/gi, '')\n    81→        .trim();\n    82→\n    83→      if (description.length < 3) {\n    84→        description = `User Story ${usId}`;\n    85→      }\n    86→\n    87→      tasks.push({\n    88→        id: `us-${usId}`,\n    89→        content: `US-${usId}: ${description}`,\n    90→        completed: isComplete,\n    91→      });\n    92→      return;\n    93→    }\n    94→\n    95→    // Pattern 3: Numbered checkboxes\n    96→    const numberedMatch = line.match(/^\\d+\\.\\s*\\[([ xX])\\]\\s*(.+)$/);\n    97→    if (numberedMatch) {\n    98→      tasks.push({\n    99→        id: `task-${index}`,\n   100→        content: numberedMatch[2].trim(),\n   101→        completed: numberedMatch[1].toLowerCase() === 'x',\n   102→      });\n   103→      return;\n   104→    }\n   105→\n   106→    // Pattern 4: Emoji status markers\n   107→    const emojiMatch = line.match(/^[-*]\\s*(✅|⬜|🔲)\\s*(.+)$/);\n   108→    if (emojiMatch) {\n   109→      tasks.push({\n   110→        id: `task-${index}`,\n   111→        content: emojiMatch[2].trim(),\n   112→        completed: emojiMatch[1] === '✅',\n   113→      });\n   114→    }\n   115→  });\n   116→\n   117→  const completed = tasks.filter((t) => t.completed).length;\n   118→  this.tasks = { tasks, completed, total: tasks.length, lastUpdated: new Date() };\n   119→  this.emit('tasks', this.tasks);\n   120→}\n   121→```\n   122→\n   123→## Required Tests\n   124→\n   125→1. **Checkbox Format Test**\n   126→   - File with `- [ ] Task` → counts as incomplete\n   127→   - File with `- [x] Task` → counts as complete\n   128→   - File with `- [X] Task` → counts as complete (case insensitive)\n   129→\n   130→2. **User Story Format Test**\n   131→   - File with `US-001: Description` → recognized as task\n   132→   - File with `US-001: Description ✅` → recognized as complete\n   133→   - Same US-XXX on multiple lines → counted only once\n   134→\n   135→3. **Mixed Format Test**\n   136→   - File with checkboxes AND user stories → all recognized\n   137→   - Total count is sum of all patterns\n   138→   - No duplicates\n   139→\n   140→4. **Real-time Update Test**\n   141→   - Modify IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md → dashboard updates immediately\n   142→   - Progress percentage updates correctly\n   143→\n   144→## Test Data\n   145→\n   146→```markdown\n   147→# Implementation Plan\n   148→\n   149→- [x] US-001: Setup project structure\n   150→- [ ] US-002: Configure TypeScript\n   151→- ✅ Configure ESLint\n   152→- ⬜ Add testing framework\n   153→\n   154→| US-003 | Add API client | TODO |\n   155→| US-004 | Implement caching | DONE |\n   156→```\n   157→\n   158→Expected: 6 tasks total, 3 complete (50%)\n   159→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"427b9edd-afbb-48fa-af22-bb40ea7675f8","uuid":"c70f67c1-d568-4b52-a8a8-bae0a9347011","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\xsams\\RalphWiggumV3\\specs\\02-implementation-plan-parser.md","content":"# Implementation Plan Parser Enhancement\n\n## User Story\n\n**As a** developer monitoring Ralph progress\n**I want** the Implementation Plan card to show accurate task counts\n**So that** I can see real progress regardless of task format used\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nThe Implementation Plan section shows \"0/0\" and \"No tasks found\" even when IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md exists with tasks. The parser only recognizes markdown checkbox format, but real plans use various formats including user stories (US-XXX), numbered lists, and emoji markers.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n### AC-1: Checkbox Format Support (Existing)\n- [ ] Recognizes `- [ ] Task description` as incomplete\n- [ ] Recognizes `- [x] Task description` as complete\n- [ ] Case insensitive for X marker\n\n### AC-2: User Story Format Support\n- [ ] Recognizes US-XXX patterns in various contexts\n- [ ] Detects completion via ✅, DONE, COMPLETE markers\n- [ ] Avoids duplicate counting for same US mentioned multiple times\n- [ ] Extracts description after US-XXX\n\n### AC-3: Numbered Checkbox Support\n- [ ] Recognizes `1. [ ] Task` format\n- [ ] Recognizes `1. [x] Task` as complete\n\n### AC-4: Emoji Status Markers\n- [ ] Recognizes `- ✅ Task` as complete\n- [ ] Recognizes `- ⬜ Task` as incomplete\n- [ ] Recognizes `- 🔲 Task` as incomplete\n\n### AC-5: Progress Display\n- [ ] Total count reflects all recognized tasks\n- [ ] Completed count reflects tasks with completion markers\n- [ ] Progress bar percentage is accurate\n- [ ] Updates in real-time when file changes\n\n## Implementation Details\n\n### File to Modify\n\n`dashboard/server/fileWatcher.ts` - parseTasks() method\n\n### Enhanced Parser Logic\n\n```typescript\nprivate async parseTasks() {\n  const content = await fs.readFile(planPath, 'utf-8');\n  const tasks: Task[] = [];\n  const lines = content.split('\\n');\n  const seenUserStories = new Set<string>();\n\n  lines.forEach((line, index) => {\n    // Pattern 1: Markdown checkboxes - [ ] or - [x]\n    const checkboxMatch = line.match(/^-\\s*\\[([ xX])\\]\\s*(.+)$/);\n    if (checkboxMatch) {\n      tasks.push({\n        id: `task-${index}`,\n        content: checkboxMatch[2].trim(),\n        completed: checkboxMatch[1].toLowerCase() === 'x',\n      });\n      return;\n    }\n\n    // Pattern 2: User Stories (US-XXX format)\n    const usMatch = line.match(/US-(\\d+)/);\n    if (usMatch) {\n      const usId = usMatch[1];\n      if (seenUserStories.has(usId)) return;\n      seenUserStories.add(usId);\n\n      const isComplete = /✅|DONE|COMPLETE|completed/i.test(line);\n      let description = line\n        .replace(/^[|\\s-]*/, '')\n        .replace(/US-\\d+[:\\s|]*/i, '')\n        .replace(/[|].*$/, '')\n        .replace(/✅|⬜|🔲|DONE|TODO|COMPLETE|PENDING/gi, '')\n        .trim();\n\n      if (description.length < 3) {\n        description = `User Story ${usId}`;\n      }\n\n      tasks.push({\n        id: `us-${usId}`,\n        content: `US-${usId}: ${description}`,\n        completed: isComplete,\n      });\n      return;\n    }\n\n    // Pattern 3: Numbered checkboxes\n    const numberedMatch = line.match(/^\\d+\\.\\s*\\[([ xX])\\]\\s*(.+)$/);\n    if (numberedMatch) {\n      tasks.push({\n        id: `task-${index}`,\n        content: numberedMatch[2].trim(),\n        completed: numberedMatch[1].toLowerCase() === 'x',\n      });\n      return;\n    }\n\n    // Pattern 4: Emoji status markers\n    const emojiMatch = line.match(/^[-*]\\s*(✅|⬜|🔲)\\s*(.+)$/);\n    if (emojiMatch) {\n      tasks.push({\n        id: `task-${index}`,\n        content: emojiMatch[2].trim(),\n        completed: emojiMatch[1] === '✅',\n      });\n    }\n  });\n\n  const completed = tasks.filter((t) => t.completed).length;\n  this.tasks = { tasks, completed, total: tasks.length, lastUpdated: new Date() };\n  this.emit('tasks', this.tasks);\n}\n```\n\n## Required Tests\n\n1. **Checkbox Format Test**\n   - File with `- [ ] Task` → counts as incomplete\n   - File with `- [x] Task` → counts as complete\n   - File with `- [X] Task` → counts as complete (case insensitive)\n\n2. **User Story Format Test**\n   - File with `US-001: Description` → recognized as task\n   - File with `US-001: Description ✅` → recognized as complete\n   - Same US-XXX on multiple lines → counted only once\n\n3. **Mixed Format Test**\n   - File with checkboxes AND user stories → all recognized\n   - Total count is sum of all patterns\n   - No duplicates\n\n4. **Real-time Update Test**\n   - Modify IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md → dashboard updates immediately\n   - Progress percentage updates correctly\n\n## Test Data\n\n```markdown\n# Implementation Plan\n\n- [x] US-001: Setup project structure\n- [ ] US-002: Configure TypeScript\n- ✅ Configure ESLint\n- ⬜ Add testing framework\n\n| US-003 | Add API client | TODO |\n| US-004 | Implement caching | DONE |\n```\n\nExpected: 6 tasks total, 3 complete (50%)\n","numLines":159,"startLine":1,"totalLines":159}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SxYpdjkad2SrFjuXf4pC5t","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# File Write Safety Instructions\n     2→\n     3→## User Story\n     4→\n     5→**As a** developer running Ralph build loops\n     6→**I want** file creation to succeed without \"File has not been read yet\" errors\n     7→**So that** my build loop doesn't fail unexpectedly when creating new files\n     8→\n     9→## Problem Statement\n    10→\n    11→When Claude Code attempts to create a new file using the Write tool, it fails with:\n    12→```\n    13→Error: File has not been read yet. Read it first before writing to it.\n    14→```\n    15→\n    16→This is a Claude Code safety mechanism that requires reading a file before writing to prevent accidental overwrites. For new files that don't exist, this creates a chicken-and-egg problem.\n    17→\n    18→## Acceptance Criteria\n    19→\n    20→### AC-1: CLAUDE.md Instructions\n    21→- [ ] CLAUDE.md contains \"File Writing Rules\" section\n    22→- [ ] Instructions explain read-before-write pattern\n    23→- [ ] Example workflow for new files provided\n    24→- [ ] Example workflow for existing files provided\n    25→\n    26→### AC-2: Build Loop Behavior\n    27→- [ ] Claude follows read→write pattern for all file operations\n    28→- [ ] New file creation succeeds on first loop iteration\n    29→- [ ] Existing file modifications see current content first\n    30→\n    31→## Implementation Details\n    32→\n    33→### File to Create/Modify\n    34→\n    35→`{TARGET_PROJECT}/CLAUDE.md` - Add this section:\n    36→\n    37→```markdown\n    38→## File Writing Rules\n    39→\n    40→**CRITICAL: Always read before write**\n    41→\n    42→Before using the Write tool to create or modify ANY file:\n    43→1. First use the Read tool to attempt reading the file\n    44→2. If the file doesn't exist, you'll get an error - that's OK\n    45→3. Then proceed with the Write tool\n    46→\n    47→This is required because Claude Code tracks which files you've read to prevent accidental overwrites.\n    48→\n    49→**Example workflow for creating a new file:**\n    50→1. Read `src/newFile.ts` → Error: file not found (expected)\n    51→2. Write `src/newFile.ts` → Success\n    52→\n    53→**Example workflow for modifying an existing file:**\n    54→1. Read `src/existingFile.ts` → Returns file content\n    55→2. Write `src/existingFile.ts` → Success\n    56→```\n    57→\n    58→### Alternative: Add to Ralph Prompts\n    59→\n    60→Add to PROMPT_build.md:\n    61→```markdown\n    62→## File Operation Safety\n    63→\n    64→When creating new files, ALWAYS attempt to read the file first using the Read tool, even if you know it doesn't exist. This satisfies Claude Code's safety check.\n    65→\n    66→Pattern:\n    67→1. Read file (may fail if new)\n    68→2. Write file (will succeed)\n    69→```\n    70→\n    71→## Required Tests\n    72→\n    73→1. **New File Creation Test**\n    74→   - Start Ralph loop that needs to create a new file\n    75→   - Verify Claude reads first (gets \"not found\")\n    76→   - Verify Claude writes successfully\n    77→   - Verify file exists with correct content\n    78→\n    79→2. **Existing File Modification Test**\n    80→   - Start Ralph loop that needs to modify an existing file\n    81→   - Verify Claude reads current content first\n    82→   - Verify Claude writes updated content\n    83→   - Verify changes are correct\n    84→\n    85→## Why This Works\n    86→\n    87→The Ralph Wiggum methodology emphasizes:\n    88→- **Self-correction**: Claude should handle errors gracefully\n    89→- **Incremental progress**: Each iteration makes progress and saves state\n    90→- **Clear completion criteria**: Know when a task is done\n    91→\n    92→By adding the \"read before write\" rule:\n    93→- Claude learns the pattern and applies it consistently\n    94→- New file creation succeeds on first attempt\n    95→- Existing file modifications are safer (you see current content first)\n    96→- The build loop doesn't fail unexpectedly\n    97→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"427b9edd-afbb-48fa-af22-bb40ea7675f8","uuid":"65d1a19c-267f-4f62-b013-f6454c200598","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\xsams\\RalphWiggumV3\\specs\\03-file-write-safety.md","content":"# File Write Safety Instructions\n\n## User Story\n\n**As a** developer running Ralph build loops\n**I want** file creation to succeed without \"File has not been read yet\" errors\n**So that** my build loop doesn't fail unexpectedly when creating new files\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nWhen Claude Code attempts to create a new file using the Write tool, it fails with:\n```\nError: File has not been read yet. Read it first before writing to it.\n```\n\nThis is a Claude Code safety mechanism that requires reading a file before writing to prevent accidental overwrites. For new files that don't exist, this creates a chicken-and-egg problem.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n### AC-1: CLAUDE.md Instructions\n- [ ] CLAUDE.md contains \"File Writing Rules\" section\n- [ ] Instructions explain read-before-write pattern\n- [ ] Example workflow for new files provided\n- [ ] Example workflow for existing files provided\n\n### AC-2: Build Loop Behavior\n- [ ] Claude follows read→write pattern for all file operations\n- [ ] New file creation succeeds on first loop iteration\n- [ ] Existing file modifications see current content first\n\n## Implementation Details\n\n### File to Create/Modify\n\n`{TARGET_PROJECT}/CLAUDE.md` - Add this section:\n\n```markdown\n## File Writing Rules\n\n**CRITICAL: Always read before write**\n\nBefore using the Write tool to create or modify ANY file:\n1. First use the Read tool to attempt reading the file\n2. If the file doesn't exist, you'll get an error - that's OK\n3. Then proceed with the Write tool\n\nThis is required because Claude Code tracks which files you've read to prevent accidental overwrites.\n\n**Example workflow for creating a new file:**\n1. Read `src/newFile.ts` → Error: file not found (expected)\n2. Write `src/newFile.ts` → Success\n\n**Example workflow for modifying an existing file:**\n1. Read `src/existingFile.ts` → Returns file content\n2. Write `src/existingFile.ts` → Success\n```\n\n### Alternative: Add to Ralph Prompts\n\nAdd to PROMPT_build.md:\n```markdown\n## File Operation Safety\n\nWhen creating new files, ALWAYS attempt to read the file first using the Read tool, even if you know it doesn't exist. This satisfies Claude Code's safety check.\n\nPattern:\n1. Read file (may fail if new)\n2. Write file (will succeed)\n```\n\n## Required Tests\n\n1. **New File Creation Test**\n   - Start Ralph loop that needs to create a new file\n   - Verify Claude reads first (gets \"not found\")\n   - Verify Claude writes successfully\n   - Verify file exists with correct content\n\n2. **Existing File Modification Test**\n   - Start Ralph loop that needs to modify an existing file\n   - Verify Claude reads current content first\n   - Verify Claude writes updated content\n   - Verify changes are correct\n\n## Why This Works\n\nThe Ralph Wiggum methodology emphasizes:\n- **Self-correction**: Claude should handle errors gracefully\n- **Incremental progress**: Each iteration makes progress and saves state\n- **Clear completion criteria**: Know when a task is done\n\nBy adding the \"read before write\" rule:\n- Claude learns the pattern and applies it consistently\n- New file creation succeeds on first attempt\n- Existing file modifications are safer (you see current content first)\n- The build loop doesn't fail unexpectedly\n","numLines":97,"startLine":1,"totalLines":97}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UTAZYBWErhdwyPTy9WiLrM","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Cross-Platform Claude CLI Compatibility\n     2→\n     3→## User Story\n     4→\n     5→**As a** developer on macOS or Linux\n     6→**I want** Ralph to work without \"--model\" flag errors\n     7→**So that** I can use Ralph Wiggum on my preferred platform\n     8→\n     9→## Problem Statement\n    10→\n    11→The `--model opus` flag works on Windows but causes an error on macOS:\n    12→```\n    13→Error: The Claude CLI doesn't support --model\n    14→```\n    15→\n    16→Different Claude CLI versions or platform builds may have different flag support.\n    17→\n    18→## Acceptance Criteria\n    19→\n    20→### AC-1: Platform Detection\n    21→- [ ] Windows uses `--model opus` flag (confirmed working)\n    22→- [ ] macOS omits flag by default\n    23→- [ ] Linux omits flag by default\n    24→\n    25→### AC-2: Environment Variable Override\n    26→- [ ] `CLAUDE_MODEL_FLAG=true` forces flag on any platform\n    27→- [ ] Useful for macOS/Linux users with newer CLI versions\n    28→\n    29→### AC-3: Consistent Behavior\n    30→- [ ] PRD generation works on all platforms\n    31→- [ ] Plan generation works on all platforms\n    32→- [ ] Build loop (loop.sh) works on all platforms\n    33→\n    34→## Implementation Details\n    35→\n    36→### Files to Modify\n    37→\n    38→| File | Changes |\n    39→|------|---------|\n    40→| `dashboard/server/planGenerator.ts` | Conditional --model flag |\n    41→| `dashboard/server/prdGenerator.ts` | Conditional --model flag |\n    42→| `loop.sh` | Conditional --model flag |\n    43→\n    44→### planGenerator.ts / prdGenerator.ts\n    45→\n    46→```typescript\n    47→// Build CLI arguments - make --model conditional for cross-platform compatibility\n    48→const claudeArgs = [\n    49→  '-p',\n    50→  '--output-format=stream-json',\n    51→  '--verbose',\n    52→  '--dangerously-skip-permissions'\n    53→];\n    54→\n    55→// Add --model flag on Windows (confirmed working) or if explicitly enabled\n    56→const isWindows = process.platform === 'win32';\n    57→if (isWindows || process.env.CLAUDE_MODEL_FLAG === 'true') {\n    58→  claudeArgs.push('--model', 'opus');\n    59→}\n    60→\n    61→this.process = spawn('claude', claudeArgs, {\n    62→  cwd: this.projectPath,\n    63→  stdio: ['pipe', 'pipe', 'pipe'],\n    64→  shell: true,\n    65→});\n    66→```\n    67→\n    68→### loop.sh\n    69→\n    70→```bash\n    71→# Detect if --model flag is supported (Windows or explicitly enabled)\n    72→MODEL_FLAG=\"\"\n    73→if [[ \"$OSTYPE\" == \"msys\"* ]] || [[ \"$OSTYPE\" == \"cygwin\"* ]] || [[ \"$OSTYPE\" == \"mingw\"* ]] || [[ \"$CLAUDE_MODEL_FLAG\" == \"true\" ]]; then\n    74→  MODEL_FLAG=\"--model opus\"\n    75→fi\n    76→\n    77→# Use $MODEL_FLAG in the claude command:\n    78→cat \"$PROMPT_FILE_PATH\" | claude -p \\\n    79→    --dangerously-skip-permissions \\\n    80→    --output-format=stream-json \\\n    81→    $MODEL_FLAG \\\n    82→    --verbose \\\n    83→    2>&1 | tee -a \"$OUTPUT_LOG\"\n    84→```\n    85→\n    86→## Behavior Summary\n    87→\n    88→| Platform | Default Behavior | With CLAUDE_MODEL_FLAG=true |\n    89→|----------|------------------|----------------------------|\n    90→| Windows | Uses --model opus | Uses --model opus |\n    91→| macOS | Omits --model flag | Uses --model opus |\n    92→| Linux | Omits --model flag | Uses --model opus |\n    93→\n    94→## Required Tests\n    95→\n    96→1. **Windows Test**\n    97→   - Run PRD generation → succeeds with model flag\n    98→   - Run Plan generation → succeeds with model flag\n    99→   - Run loop.sh → succeeds with model flag\n   100→\n   101→2. **macOS Test (or simulated)**\n   102→   - Run PRD generation → succeeds without model flag\n   103→   - Run Plan generation → succeeds without model flag\n   104→\n   105→3. **Override Test**\n   106→   - Set `CLAUDE_MODEL_FLAG=true`\n   107→   - Run on non-Windows → uses model flag\n   108→\n   109→## Environment Variable Usage\n   110→\n   111→```bash\n   112→# macOS/Linux users with newer Claude CLI can opt-in:\n   113→export CLAUDE_MODEL_FLAG=true\n   114→npm run dev\n   115→\n   116→# Or inline:\n   117→CLAUDE_MODEL_FLAG=true npm run dev\n   118→```\n   119→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"427b9edd-afbb-48fa-af22-bb40ea7675f8","uuid":"62b35f85-c021-4124-87b8-2af9bcffcc42","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\xsams\\RalphWiggumV3\\specs\\04-cross-platform-cli.md","content":"# Cross-Platform Claude CLI Compatibility\n\n## User Story\n\n**As a** developer on macOS or Linux\n**I want** Ralph to work without \"--model\" flag errors\n**So that** I can use Ralph Wiggum on my preferred platform\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nThe `--model opus` flag works on Windows but causes an error on macOS:\n```\nError: The Claude CLI doesn't support --model\n```\n\nDifferent Claude CLI versions or platform builds may have different flag support.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n### AC-1: Platform Detection\n- [ ] Windows uses `--model opus` flag (confirmed working)\n- [ ] macOS omits flag by default\n- [ ] Linux omits flag by default\n\n### AC-2: Environment Variable Override\n- [ ] `CLAUDE_MODEL_FLAG=true` forces flag on any platform\n- [ ] Useful for macOS/Linux users with newer CLI versions\n\n### AC-3: Consistent Behavior\n- [ ] PRD generation works on all platforms\n- [ ] Plan generation works on all platforms\n- [ ] Build loop (loop.sh) works on all platforms\n\n## Implementation Details\n\n### Files to Modify\n\n| File | Changes |\n|------|---------|\n| `dashboard/server/planGenerator.ts` | Conditional --model flag |\n| `dashboard/server/prdGenerator.ts` | Conditional --model flag |\n| `loop.sh` | Conditional --model flag |\n\n### planGenerator.ts / prdGenerator.ts\n\n```typescript\n// Build CLI arguments - make --model conditional for cross-platform compatibility\nconst claudeArgs = [\n  '-p',\n  '--output-format=stream-json',\n  '--verbose',\n  '--dangerously-skip-permissions'\n];\n\n// Add --model flag on Windows (confirmed working) or if explicitly enabled\nconst isWindows = process.platform === 'win32';\nif (isWindows || process.env.CLAUDE_MODEL_FLAG === 'true') {\n  claudeArgs.push('--model', 'opus');\n}\n\nthis.process = spawn('claude', claudeArgs, {\n  cwd: this.projectPath,\n  stdio: ['pipe', 'pipe', 'pipe'],\n  shell: true,\n});\n```\n\n### loop.sh\n\n```bash\n# Detect if --model flag is supported (Windows or explicitly enabled)\nMODEL_FLAG=\"\"\nif [[ \"$OSTYPE\" == \"msys\"* ]] || [[ \"$OSTYPE\" == \"cygwin\"* ]] || [[ \"$OSTYPE\" == \"mingw\"* ]] || [[ \"$CLAUDE_MODEL_FLAG\" == \"true\" ]]; then\n  MODEL_FLAG=\"--model opus\"\nfi\n\n# Use $MODEL_FLAG in the claude command:\ncat \"$PROMPT_FILE_PATH\" | claude -p \\\n    --dangerously-skip-permissions \\\n    --output-format=stream-json \\\n    $MODEL_FLAG \\\n    --verbose \\\n    2>&1 | tee -a \"$OUTPUT_LOG\"\n```\n\n## Behavior Summary\n\n| Platform | Default Behavior | With CLAUDE_MODEL_FLAG=true |\n|----------|------------------|----------------------------|\n| Windows | Uses --model opus | Uses --model opus |\n| macOS | Omits --model flag | Uses --model opus |\n| Linux | Omits --model flag | Uses --model opus |\n\n## Required Tests\n\n1. **Windows Test**\n   - Run PRD generation → succeeds with model flag\n   - Run Plan generation → succeeds with model flag\n   - Run loop.sh → succeeds with model flag\n\n2. **macOS Test (or simulated)**\n   - Run PRD generation → succeeds without model flag\n   - Run Plan generation → succeeds without model flag\n\n3. **Override Test**\n   - Set `CLAUDE_MODEL_FLAG=true`\n   - Run on non-Windows → uses model flag\n\n## Environment Variable Usage\n\n```bash\n# macOS/Linux users with newer Claude CLI can opt-in:\nexport CLAUDE_MODEL_FLAG=true\nnpm run dev\n\n# Or inline:\nCLAUDE_MODEL_FLAG=true npm run dev\n```\n","numLines":119,"startLine":1,"totalLines":119}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AfyjgJqLb9qb1FkKSXB7U7","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Agent Installation from Dashboard\n     2→\n     3→## User Story\n     4→\n     5→**As a** developer using Ralph Wiggum\n     6→**I want to** install specialist agents from the dashboard with one click\n     7→**So that** Claude can delegate to specialized agents during the build loop\n     8→\n     9→## Problem Statement\n    10→\n    11→Users need to:\n    12→1. Create agent definition files in the repo so they're committed to GitHub\n    13→2. Install agents globally (~/.claude/agents/) with a button click\n    14→3. Install agents to project (.claude/agents/) with a button click\n    15→4. Have agents ready to use after cloning the repo\n    16→\n    17→Currently, there's no UI for agent installation and no agent files exist in the repo.\n    18→\n    19→## Acceptance Criteria\n    20→\n    21→### AC-1: Agent Definition Files\n    22→- [ ] At least 4 agent files exist in RalphWiggumV2/.claude/agents/\n    23→- [ ] Each file has YAML frontmatter with name and description\n    24→- [ ] Files are committed to git\n    25→- [ ] Agents cover: React/TS, Accessibility, QoL UX, Go Backend\n    26→\n    27→### AC-2: List Repo Agents\n    28→- [ ] Dashboard shows agents available in Ralph repo\n    29→- [ ] \"Available for Installation\" section distinct from installed agents\n    30→- [ ] Shows agent name, description, and source badge\n    31→\n    32→### AC-3: Install to Global\n    33→- [ ] \"Global\" button installs agent to ~/.claude/agents/\n    34→- [ ] Button shows loading state during installation\n    35→- [ ] Button disabled after successful install\n    36→- [ ] Available agents list refreshes after install\n    37→\n    38→### AC-4: Install to Project\n    39→- [ ] \"Project\" button installs agent to .claude/agents/\n    40→- [ ] Creates directory if it doesn't exist\n    41→- [ ] Button shows loading state during installation\n    42→- [ ] Available agents list refreshes after install\n    43→\n    44→### AC-5: Install All\n    45→- [ ] \"Install All Global\" button in header\n    46→- [ ] Installs all repo agents to global location\n    47→- [ ] Shows loading state during operation\n    48→\n    49→## Implementation Details\n    50→\n    51→### Agent Files to Create\n    52→\n    53→`RalphWiggumV2/.claude/agents/react-typescript-expert.md`:\n    54→```markdown\n    55→---\n    56→name: React TypeScript Expert\n    57→description: React architecture, hooks, state, TypeScript types, code reviews, performance optimization\n    58→---\n    59→\n    60→## Role\n    61→You are a React and TypeScript expert specializing in modern React patterns.\n    62→\n    63→## Responsibilities\n    64→- Review React component architecture\n    65→- Optimize hooks usage and state management\n    66→- Ensure TypeScript types are properly defined\n    67→- Identify performance bottlenecks\n    68→- Suggest best practices for React 18+ features\n    69→```\n    70→\n    71→Similar files for:\n    72→- `accessibility-expert.md`\n    73→- `qol-ux-expert.md`\n    74→- `golang-backend-expert.md`\n    75→\n    76→### Files to Modify\n    77→\n    78→| File | Changes |\n    79→|------|---------|\n    80→| `dashboard/server/projectConfig.ts` | Add installAgentGlobal, installAgentProject, listRepoAgents methods |\n    81→| `dashboard/server/index.ts` | Add WebSocket handlers for install commands |\n    82→| `dashboard/src/types/index.ts` | Add AgentInfo 'repo' source, command types |\n    83→| `dashboard/src/hooks/useWebSocket.ts` | Add state and callbacks for installation |\n    84→| `dashboard/src/components/setup/SpecialistAgentsConfig.tsx` | Add install buttons, repo agents section |\n    85→| `dashboard/src/components/Dashboard.tsx` | Wire up new props |\n    86→\n    87→### Key Methods in projectConfig.ts\n    88→\n    89→```typescript\n    90→async installAgentGlobal(agentId: string): Promise<void> {\n    91→  const sourcePath = path.join(this.ralphPath, '.claude', 'agents', `${agentId}.md`);\n    92→  const globalDir = path.join(os.homedir(), '.claude', 'agents');\n    93→  const destPath = path.join(globalDir, `${agentId}.md`);\n    94→  \n    95→  await fs.mkdir(globalDir, { recursive: true });\n    96→  const content = await fs.readFile(sourcePath, 'utf-8');\n    97→  await fs.writeFile(destPath, content, 'utf-8');\n    98→}\n    99→\n   100→async installAgentProject(agentId: string): Promise<void> {\n   101→  const sourcePath = path.join(this.ralphPath, '.claude', 'agents', `${agentId}.md`);\n   102→  const projectDir = path.join(this.projectPath, '.claude', 'agents');\n   103→  const destPath = path.join(projectDir, `${agentId}.md`);\n   104→  \n   105→  await fs.mkdir(projectDir, { recursive: true });\n   106→  const content = await fs.readFile(sourcePath, 'utf-8');\n   107→  await fs.writeFile(destPath, content, 'utf-8');\n   108→}\n   109→\n   110→async listRepoAgents(): Promise<AgentInfo[]> {\n   111→  const agentsDir = path.join(this.ralphPath, '.claude', 'agents');\n   112→  // Parse each .md file and return AgentInfo array\n   113→}\n   114→```\n   115→\n   116→## UI Mockup\n   117→\n   118→```\n   119→┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n   120→│ Specialist Agents                              [Install All Global] │\n   121→├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n   122→│                                                                   │\n   123→│ ┌─ Available for Installation ─────────────────────────────────┐ │\n   124→│ │  from RalphWiggumV2/.claude/agents/                          │ │\n   125→│ │                                                               │ │\n   126→│ │  [Icon] React TypeScript Expert           [Global] [Project] │ │\n   127→│ │         React architecture, hooks...      (buttons)          │ │\n   128→│ │                                                               │ │\n   129→│ │  [Icon] Accessibility Expert              [Installed] [Project] │\n   130→│ │         WCAG 2.2 compliance...            (disabled)         │ │\n   131→│ └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │\n   132→│                                                                   │\n   133→│ ┌─ Global Agents ──────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │\n   134→│ │  ~/.claude/agents/                                           │ │\n   135→│ │                                                               │ │\n   136→│ │  [Icon] Accessibility Expert     [Global] [Enabled]  [Toggle] │ │\n   137→│ └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │\n   138→└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n   139→```\n   140→\n   141→## Required Tests\n   142→\n   143→1. **Agent Files Exist Test**\n   144→   - Verify 4 agent files in RalphWiggumV2/.claude/agents/\n   145→   - Each has valid YAML frontmatter\n   146→   - Files are tracked by git\n   147→\n   148→2. **Install Global Test**\n   149→   - Click \"Global\" button for an agent\n   150→   - Verify file appears in ~/.claude/agents/\n   151→   - Verify agent moves to \"Global Agents\" section\n   152→\n   153→3. **Install Project Test**\n   154→   - Click \"Project\" button for an agent\n   155→   - Verify file appears in .claude/agents/\n   156→   - Verify directory created if needed\n   157→\n   158→4. **Install All Test**\n   159→   - Click \"Install All Global\"\n   160→   - Verify all repo agents copied to global location\n   161→\n   162→5. **Clone Workflow Test**\n   163→   - Clone repo to new location\n   164→   - Open dashboard\n   165→   - Verify repo agents appear in \"Available for Installation\"\n   166→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"427b9edd-afbb-48fa-af22-bb40ea7675f8","uuid":"50aa41f0-ad52-43d7-9ad4-41d99a64a6f3","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\xsams\\RalphWiggumV3\\specs\\05-agent-installation.md","content":"# Agent Installation from Dashboard\n\n## User Story\n\n**As a** developer using Ralph Wiggum\n**I want to** install specialist agents from the dashboard with one click\n**So that** Claude can delegate to specialized agents during the build loop\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nUsers need to:\n1. Create agent definition files in the repo so they're committed to GitHub\n2. Install agents globally (~/.claude/agents/) with a button click\n3. Install agents to project (.claude/agents/) with a button click\n4. Have agents ready to use after cloning the repo\n\nCurrently, there's no UI for agent installation and no agent files exist in the repo.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n### AC-1: Agent Definition Files\n- [ ] At least 4 agent files exist in RalphWiggumV2/.claude/agents/\n- [ ] Each file has YAML frontmatter with name and description\n- [ ] Files are committed to git\n- [ ] Agents cover: React/TS, Accessibility, QoL UX, Go Backend\n\n### AC-2: List Repo Agents\n- [ ] Dashboard shows agents available in Ralph repo\n- [ ] \"Available for Installation\" section distinct from installed agents\n- [ ] Shows agent name, description, and source badge\n\n### AC-3: Install to Global\n- [ ] \"Global\" button installs agent to ~/.claude/agents/\n- [ ] Button shows loading state during installation\n- [ ] Button disabled after successful install\n- [ ] Available agents list refreshes after install\n\n### AC-4: Install to Project\n- [ ] \"Project\" button installs agent to .claude/agents/\n- [ ] Creates directory if it doesn't exist\n- [ ] Button shows loading state during installation\n- [ ] Available agents list refreshes after install\n\n### AC-5: Install All\n- [ ] \"Install All Global\" button in header\n- [ ] Installs all repo agents to global location\n- [ ] Shows loading state during operation\n\n## Implementation Details\n\n### Agent Files to Create\n\n`RalphWiggumV2/.claude/agents/react-typescript-expert.md`:\n```markdown\n---\nname: React TypeScript Expert\ndescription: React architecture, hooks, state, TypeScript types, code reviews, performance optimization\n---\n\n## Role\nYou are a React and TypeScript expert specializing in modern React patterns.\n\n## Responsibilities\n- Review React component architecture\n- Optimize hooks usage and state management\n- Ensure TypeScript types are properly defined\n- Identify performance bottlenecks\n- Suggest best practices for React 18+ features\n```\n\nSimilar files for:\n- `accessibility-expert.md`\n- `qol-ux-expert.md`\n- `golang-backend-expert.md`\n\n### Files to Modify\n\n| File | Changes |\n|------|---------|\n| `dashboard/server/projectConfig.ts` | Add installAgentGlobal, installAgentProject, listRepoAgents methods |\n| `dashboard/server/index.ts` | Add WebSocket handlers for install commands |\n| `dashboard/src/types/index.ts` | Add AgentInfo 'repo' source, command types |\n| `dashboard/src/hooks/useWebSocket.ts` | Add state and callbacks for installation |\n| `dashboard/src/components/setup/SpecialistAgentsConfig.tsx` | Add install buttons, repo agents section |\n| `dashboard/src/components/Dashboard.tsx` | Wire up new props |\n\n### Key Methods in projectConfig.ts\n\n```typescript\nasync installAgentGlobal(agentId: string): Promise<void> {\n  const sourcePath = path.join(this.ralphPath, '.claude', 'agents', `${agentId}.md`);\n  const globalDir = path.join(os.homedir(), '.claude', 'agents');\n  const destPath = path.join(globalDir, `${agentId}.md`);\n  \n  await fs.mkdir(globalDir, { recursive: true });\n  const content = await fs.readFile(sourcePath, 'utf-8');\n  await fs.writeFile(destPath, content, 'utf-8');\n}\n\nasync installAgentProject(agentId: string): Promise<void> {\n  const sourcePath = path.join(this.ralphPath, '.claude', 'agents', `${agentId}.md`);\n  const projectDir = path.join(this.projectPath, '.claude', 'agents');\n  const destPath = path.join(projectDir, `${agentId}.md`);\n  \n  await fs.mkdir(projectDir, { recursive: true });\n  const content = await fs.readFile(sourcePath, 'utf-8');\n  await fs.writeFile(destPath, content, 'utf-8');\n}\n\nasync listRepoAgents(): Promise<AgentInfo[]> {\n  const agentsDir = path.join(this.ralphPath, '.claude', 'agents');\n  // Parse each .md file and return AgentInfo array\n}\n```\n\n## UI Mockup\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Specialist Agents                              [Install All Global] │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│                                                                   │\n│ ┌─ Available for Installation ─────────────────────────────────┐ │\n│ │  from RalphWiggumV2/.claude/agents/                          │ │\n│ │                                                               │ │\n│ │  [Icon] React TypeScript Expert           [Global] [Project] │ │\n│ │         React architecture, hooks...      (buttons)          │ │\n│ │                                                               │ │\n│ │  [Icon] Accessibility Expert              [Installed] [Project] │\n│ │         WCAG 2.2 compliance...            (disabled)         │ │\n│ └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │\n│                                                                   │\n│ ┌─ Global Agents ──────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │\n│ │  ~/.claude/agents/                                           │ │\n│ │                                                               │ │\n│ │  [Icon] Accessibility Expert     [Global] [Enabled]  [Toggle] │ │\n│ └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n## Required Tests\n\n1. **Agent Files Exist Test**\n   - Verify 4 agent files in RalphWiggumV2/.claude/agents/\n   - Each has valid YAML frontmatter\n   - Files are tracked by git\n\n2. **Install Global Test**\n   - Click \"Global\" button for an agent\n   - Verify file appears in ~/.claude/agents/\n   - Verify agent moves to \"Global Agents\" section\n\n3. **Install Project Test**\n   - Click \"Project\" button for an agent\n   - Verify file appears in .claude/agents/\n   - Verify directory created if needed\n\n4. **Install All Test**\n   - Click \"Install All Global\"\n   - Verify all repo agents copied to global location\n\n5. **Clone Workflow Test**\n   - Clone repo to new location\n   - Open dashboard\n   - Verify repo agents appear in \"Available for Installation\"\n","numLines":166,"startLine":1,"totalLines":166}}}
