{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vj2GeaTBJ6CCAV9Rijo9vt","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Analysis of App.tsx Structure\n\nBased on my analysis of the codebase, here's the current structure and routing mechanism:\n\n### Current Structure\n\n**File**: `C:\\Users\\xsams\\RalphWiggumV3\\RalphWiggumV2\\dashboard\\src\\App.tsx`\n\nThe App.tsx file is extremely minimal:\n- **9 lines total**\n- Imports the Dashboard component\n- Simply returns `<Dashboard />` with no routing logic\n- No router library is used\n\n### Current Navigation Mechanism\n\nThe application uses **tab-based navigation** within the Dashboard component instead of URL routing:\n\n1. **State-based tabs** using React's `useState`:\n   ```tsx\n   const [activeTab, setActiveTab] = useState('dashboard');\n   ```\n\n2. **Radix UI Tabs component** (`@radix-ui/react-tabs`) for the UI:\n   - 4 main tabs: Dashboard, Generate, Logs, Setup\n   - Nested tabs within Generate: Plan and PRD\n   - Tab switching happens via `onValueChange={setActiveTab}`\n\n3. **Conditional rendering** based on tab state:\n   - Dashboard tab: Shows LoopControls, ExistingDocsViewer, status grid, tasks\n   - Generate tab: Shows PlanGenerator and PRDGenerator in nested tabs\n   - Logs tab: Shows LogViewer\n   - Setup tab: Shows SetupWizard\n\n4. **Onboarding wizard** is conditionally rendered:\n   - Uses `showOnboarding` state\n   - Completely replaces Dashboard when active\n   - No URL routing involved\n\n### No Router Library\n\nThe `package.json` confirms there is **no router library installed** (no react-router-dom, wouter, tanstack-router, etc.).\n\n### Recommendations for Adding Launcher/Dashboard Routing\n\nTo implement routing between launcher and dashboard views, you have several options:\n\n1. **Minimal Approach (State-based)**:\n   - Add a top-level state in App.tsx to toggle between \"launcher\" and \"dashboard\" views\n   - Similar to the existing onboarding pattern\n   - No additional dependencies needed\n\n2. **URL-based Routing (Recommended)**:\n   - Install a routing library like `react-router-dom` or `wouter` (lightweight)\n   - Create routes for `/launcher` and `/dashboard`\n   - Allows browser history and bookmarking\n   - Better user experience with back/forward buttons\n\n3. **Hybrid Approach**:\n   - Use state-based routing for the launcher/dashboard split\n   - Keep existing tab-based navigation within Dashboard\n   - Minimal changes to existing code\n\nThe current architecture is well-suited for adding a router at the App.tsx level since Dashboard is already a self-contained component. You would create a Launcher component and route between them."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a50ce91 (for resuming to continue this agent's work if needed)"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2114ed30-b19e-4096-a327-936d9cd9d573","uuid":"1c0e4d4c-d0fd-4c43-93c2-b3587e700f3a","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Read the file C:\\Users\\xsams\\RalphWiggumV3\\RalphWiggumV2\\dashboard\\src\\App.tsx and describe its current structure, routing mechanism (if any), and how it renders the Dashboard. We need to understand how to modify it to route between launcher and dashboard views.","agentId":"a50ce91","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Analysis of App.tsx Structure\n\nBased on my analysis of the codebase, here's the current structure and routing mechanism:\n\n### Current Structure\n\n**File**: `C:\\Users\\xsams\\RalphWiggumV3\\RalphWiggumV2\\dashboard\\src\\App.tsx`\n\nThe App.tsx file is extremely minimal:\n- **9 lines total**\n- Imports the Dashboard component\n- Simply returns `<Dashboard />` with no routing logic\n- No router library is used\n\n### Current Navigation Mechanism\n\nThe application uses **tab-based navigation** within the Dashboard component instead of URL routing:\n\n1. **State-based tabs** using React's `useState`:\n   ```tsx\n   const [activeTab, setActiveTab] = useState('dashboard');\n   ```\n\n2. **Radix UI Tabs component** (`@radix-ui/react-tabs`) for the UI:\n   - 4 main tabs: Dashboard, Generate, Logs, Setup\n   - Nested tabs within Generate: Plan and PRD\n   - Tab switching happens via `onValueChange={setActiveTab}`\n\n3. **Conditional rendering** based on tab state:\n   - Dashboard tab: Shows LoopControls, ExistingDocsViewer, status grid, tasks\n   - Generate tab: Shows PlanGenerator and PRDGenerator in nested tabs\n   - Logs tab: Shows LogViewer\n   - Setup tab: Shows SetupWizard\n\n4. **Onboarding wizard** is conditionally rendered:\n   - Uses `showOnboarding` state\n   - Completely replaces Dashboard when active\n   - No URL routing involved\n\n### No Router Library\n\nThe `package.json` confirms there is **no router library installed** (no react-router-dom, wouter, tanstack-router, etc.).\n\n### Recommendations for Adding Launcher/Dashboard Routing\n\nTo implement routing between launcher and dashboard views, you have several options:\n\n1. **Minimal Approach (State-based)**:\n   - Add a top-level state in App.tsx to toggle between \"launcher\" and \"dashboard\" views\n   - Similar to the existing onboarding pattern\n   - No additional dependencies needed\n\n2. **URL-based Routing (Recommended)**:\n   - Install a routing library like `react-router-dom` or `wouter` (lightweight)\n   - Create routes for `/launcher` and `/dashboard`\n   - Allows browser history and bookmarking\n   - Better user experience with back/forward buttons\n\n3. **Hybrid Approach**:\n   - Use state-based routing for the launcher/dashboard split\n   - Keep existing tab-based navigation within Dashboard\n   - Minimal changes to existing code\n\nThe current architecture is well-suited for adding a router at the App.tsx level since Dashboard is already a self-contained component. You would create a Launcher component and route between them."}],"totalDurationMs":38098,"totalTokens":19755,"totalToolUseCount":3,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1348,"cache_read_input_tokens":17749,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1348,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":655,"service_tier":"standard"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QALNfNxRriqrSzcsunkTf6","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Complete Summary of Project Launcher Feature Set (09-project-launcher.md)\n\n## Overview\nThe Project Launcher is a multi-project orchestration system that allows developers to manage multiple Ralph Wiggum projects from a single interface, enabling parallel loops and easy switching between project dashboards.\n\n---\n\n## User Story\n**As a** developer working on multiple projects  \n**I want to** manage all my Ralph Wiggum projects from a single launcher  \n**So that** I can run parallel loops and switch between project dashboards easily\n\n---\n\n## Problem Statement\nCurrently, using Ralph Wiggum requires:\n1. Navigating to each project directory separately\n2. Running the dashboard manually\n3. Managing separate terminal sessions and ports for multiple projects\n\nThis makes it difficult to work on multiple projects simultaneously and track their status.\n\n---\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n### AC-1: Project List View\n- Launcher displays all registered projects in card format\n- Each card shows: project name, path, Ralph-ready status, loop status\n- Cards have action buttons: Open Dashboard, Start/Stop, Remove\n\n### AC-2: Add Project\n- \"Add Project\" button opens folder browser dialog\n- Selected folder is validated and added to project list\n- Project list persists across launcher restarts (stored in `~/.ralph/projects.json`)\n\n### AC-3: Open Dashboard\n- \"Open Dashboard\" button spawns a new dashboard instance for that project\n- Dashboard runs on dynamically allocated ports\n- New browser tab opens automatically with the project's dashboard\n\n### AC-4: Parallel Instances\n- Multiple dashboard instances can run simultaneously\n- Each instance manages its own project directory\n- Launcher shows which instances are running\n\n### AC-5: Instance Management\n- \"Stop\" button terminates a running instance\n- Launcher tracks instance status in real-time\n- Crashed instances are detected and reported\n\n### AC-6: Auto-Discovery\n- \"Discover Projects\" scans common directories for git repos\n- Shows suggestions for projects to add\n- Indicates which suggested projects are Ralph-ready\n\n---\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe launcher runs on port 5173 and spawns separate dashboard instances on dynamically allocated ports:\n\n```\nRALPH LAUNCHER (port 5173)\n  └── Spawns separate processes:\n      ├── Dashboard A (Port 5174/3002) → browser tab\n      ├── Dashboard B (Port 5175/3003) → browser tab\n      └── Dashboard C (Port 5176/3004) → browser tab\n```\n\n---\n\n## Files to Create\n\n| File | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| `server/projectRegistry.ts` | CRUD for project list, stored in ~/.ralph/projects.json |\n| `server/instanceSpawner.ts` | Spawns dashboard backends on different ports |\n| `server/portManager.ts` | Finds available ports dynamically |\n| `server/projectDiscovery.ts` | Scans directories for git/Ralph projects |\n| `src/components/launcher/LauncherHome.tsx` | Main launcher view |\n| `src/components/launcher/ProjectCard.tsx` | Individual project card |\n| `src/components/launcher/AddProjectDialog.tsx` | Folder picker dialog |\n| `src/hooks/useLauncher.ts` | Launcher state management hook |\n\n---\n\n## Data Structures\n\n### Project Interface\n```typescript\ninterface Project {\n  id: string;\n  path: string;\n  name: string;\n  addedAt: Date;\n  lastOpened?: Date;\n  isRalphReady: boolean;\n}\n```\n\n### Instance Interface\n```typescript\ninterface Instance {\n  projectId: string;\n  backendPort: number;\n  frontendPort: number;\n  pid: number;\n  startedAt: Date;\n  loopStatus?: {\n    running: boolean;\n    iteration: number;\n    mode: string;\n  };\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## WebSocket Message Types\n\n### Project Management\n- `{ type: 'launcher:projects:list' }` - Request list of all projects\n- `{ type: 'launcher:projects:add', payload: { path: string } }` - Add new project\n- `{ type: 'launcher:projects:remove', payload: { projectId: string } }` - Remove project\n\n### Instance Management\n- `{ type: 'launcher:instance:spawn', payload: { projectId: string } }` - Spawn dashboard instance\n- `{ type: 'launcher:instance:stop', payload: { projectId: string } }` - Stop running instance\n- `{ type: 'launcher:instances:list' }` - Request list of running instances\n\n### Auto-Discovery\n- `{ type: 'launcher:discover' }` - Trigger project auto-discovery\n\n---\n\n## UI Components\n\nThe launcher displays project cards with the following information:\n- Project name and path\n- Status indicator (Running, Idle, Setup Required)\n- Loop progress (iteration count, task completion)\n- Action buttons (Open Dashboard, Start/Stop, Remove, Setup)\n- Visual indicators for Ralph-ready status\n\nHeader includes:\n- \"Discover\" button for auto-discovery\n- \"+ Add Project\" button for manual addition\n\n---\n\n## Required Tests\n\n1. **Project CRUD Test**\n   - Add project → appears in list\n   - Remove project → disappears from list\n   - Restart launcher → projects persist\n\n2. **Instance Spawning Test**\n   - Click \"Open Dashboard\" → backend starts on new port\n   - Browser tab opens → dashboard loads\n   - Dashboard connects to correct backend\n\n3. **Parallel Instances Test**\n   - Open 3 projects simultaneously\n   - All 3 dashboards work independently\n   - Stopping one doesn't affect others\n\n4. **Port Allocation Test**\n   - First instance gets 3002/5174\n   - Second instance gets 3003/5175\n   - Port conflicts are avoided\n\n5. **Auto-Discovery Test**\n   - Scans ~/Projects, ~/code, etc.\n   - Finds git repositories\n   - Identifies Ralph-ready projects\n\n---\n\n## Dependencies\n\n- Child process spawning (Node.js built-in)\n- Port availability checking (net module)\n- File system access for project discovery\n- Browser tab opening (window.open())\n\n---\n\n## Cross-Platform Notes\n\n### Windows\n- Use Git Bash for loop.sh execution\n- Spawn with `shell: true`\n- Backslash paths\n\n### macOS/Linux\n- Direct bash execution\n- Forward slash paths\n- Native process handling\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nThe Project Launcher feature enables centralized management of multiple Ralph Wiggum projects through a single interface. It handles dynamic port allocation, parallel instance execution, project persistence, and auto-discovery capabilities. The implementation requires 8 new files spanning server-side logic (registry, spawner, port manager, discovery) and client-side UI components (launcher home, project cards, dialogs, hooks). The WebSocket protocol defines 7 message types for managing projects and instances."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a1f2b6e (for resuming to continue this agent's work if needed)"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2114ed30-b19e-4096-a327-936d9cd9d573","uuid":"5c83cd8f-e31f-479a-a56d-4fa8308c7a56","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Read the file at C:\\Users\\xsams\\RalphWiggumV3\\specs\\09-project-launcher.md and provide a complete summary of all requirements, acceptance criteria, and implementation details for the Project Launcher feature set. Include all WebSocket message types, UI components, and data structures specified.","agentId":"a1f2b6e","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Complete Summary of Project Launcher Feature Set (09-project-launcher.md)\n\n## Overview\nThe Project Launcher is a multi-project orchestration system that allows developers to manage multiple Ralph Wiggum projects from a single interface, enabling parallel loops and easy switching between project dashboards.\n\n---\n\n## User Story\n**As a** developer working on multiple projects  \n**I want to** manage all my Ralph Wiggum projects from a single launcher  \n**So that** I can run parallel loops and switch between project dashboards easily\n\n---\n\n## Problem Statement\nCurrently, using Ralph Wiggum requires:\n1. Navigating to each project directory separately\n2. Running the dashboard manually\n3. Managing separate terminal sessions and ports for multiple projects\n\nThis makes it difficult to work on multiple projects simultaneously and track their status.\n\n---\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n### AC-1: Project List View\n- Launcher displays all registered projects in card format\n- Each card shows: project name, path, Ralph-ready status, loop status\n- Cards have action buttons: Open Dashboard, Start/Stop, Remove\n\n### AC-2: Add Project\n- \"Add Project\" button opens folder browser dialog\n- Selected folder is validated and added to project list\n- Project list persists across launcher restarts (stored in `~/.ralph/projects.json`)\n\n### AC-3: Open Dashboard\n- \"Open Dashboard\" button spawns a new dashboard instance for that project\n- Dashboard runs on dynamically allocated ports\n- New browser tab opens automatically with the project's dashboard\n\n### AC-4: Parallel Instances\n- Multiple dashboard instances can run simultaneously\n- Each instance manages its own project directory\n- Launcher shows which instances are running\n\n### AC-5: Instance Management\n- \"Stop\" button terminates a running instance\n- Launcher tracks instance status in real-time\n- Crashed instances are detected and reported\n\n### AC-6: Auto-Discovery\n- \"Discover Projects\" scans common directories for git repos\n- Shows suggestions for projects to add\n- Indicates which suggested projects are Ralph-ready\n\n---\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe launcher runs on port 5173 and spawns separate dashboard instances on dynamically allocated ports:\n\n```\nRALPH LAUNCHER (port 5173)\n  └── Spawns separate processes:\n      ├── Dashboard A (Port 5174/3002) → browser tab\n      ├── Dashboard B (Port 5175/3003) → browser tab\n      └── Dashboard C (Port 5176/3004) → browser tab\n```\n\n---\n\n## Files to Create\n\n| File | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| `server/projectRegistry.ts` | CRUD for project list, stored in ~/.ralph/projects.json |\n| `server/instanceSpawner.ts` | Spawns dashboard backends on different ports |\n| `server/portManager.ts` | Finds available ports dynamically |\n| `server/projectDiscovery.ts` | Scans directories for git/Ralph projects |\n| `src/components/launcher/LauncherHome.tsx` | Main launcher view |\n| `src/components/launcher/ProjectCard.tsx` | Individual project card |\n| `src/components/launcher/AddProjectDialog.tsx` | Folder picker dialog |\n| `src/hooks/useLauncher.ts` | Launcher state management hook |\n\n---\n\n## Data Structures\n\n### Project Interface\n```typescript\ninterface Project {\n  id: string;\n  path: string;\n  name: string;\n  addedAt: Date;\n  lastOpened?: Date;\n  isRalphReady: boolean;\n}\n```\n\n### Instance Interface\n```typescript\ninterface Instance {\n  projectId: string;\n  backendPort: number;\n  frontendPort: number;\n  pid: number;\n  startedAt: Date;\n  loopStatus?: {\n    running: boolean;\n    iteration: number;\n    mode: string;\n  };\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## WebSocket Message Types\n\n### Project Management\n- `{ type: 'launcher:projects:list' }` - Request list of all projects\n- `{ type: 'launcher:projects:add', payload: { path: string } }` - Add new project\n- `{ type: 'launcher:projects:remove', payload: { projectId: string } }` - Remove project\n\n### Instance Management\n- `{ type: 'launcher:instance:spawn', payload: { projectId: string } }` - Spawn dashboard instance\n- `{ type: 'launcher:instance:stop', payload: { projectId: string } }` - Stop running instance\n- `{ type: 'launcher:instances:list' }` - Request list of running instances\n\n### Auto-Discovery\n- `{ type: 'launcher:discover' }` - Trigger project auto-discovery\n\n---\n\n## UI Components\n\nThe launcher displays project cards with the following information:\n- Project name and path\n- Status indicator (Running, Idle, Setup Required)\n- Loop progress (iteration count, task completion)\n- Action buttons (Open Dashboard, Start/Stop, Remove, Setup)\n- Visual indicators for Ralph-ready status\n\nHeader includes:\n- \"Discover\" button for auto-discovery\n- \"+ Add Project\" button for manual addition\n\n---\n\n## Required Tests\n\n1. **Project CRUD Test**\n   - Add project → appears in list\n   - Remove project → disappears from list\n   - Restart launcher → projects persist\n\n2. **Instance Spawning Test**\n   - Click \"Open Dashboard\" → backend starts on new port\n   - Browser tab opens → dashboard loads\n   - Dashboard connects to correct backend\n\n3. **Parallel Instances Test**\n   - Open 3 projects simultaneously\n   - All 3 dashboards work independently\n   - Stopping one doesn't affect others\n\n4. **Port Allocation Test**\n   - First instance gets 3002/5174\n   - Second instance gets 3003/5175\n   - Port conflicts are avoided\n\n5. **Auto-Discovery Test**\n   - Scans ~/Projects, ~/code, etc.\n   - Finds git repositories\n   - Identifies Ralph-ready projects\n\n---\n\n## Dependencies\n\n- Child process spawning (Node.js built-in)\n- Port availability checking (net module)\n- File system access for project discovery\n- Browser tab opening (window.open())\n\n---\n\n## Cross-Platform Notes\n\n### Windows\n- Use Git Bash for loop.sh execution\n- Spawn with `shell: true`\n- Backslash paths\n\n### macOS/Linux\n- Direct bash execution\n- Forward slash paths\n- Native process handling\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nThe Project Launcher feature enables centralized management of multiple Ralph Wiggum projects through a single interface. It handles dynamic port allocation, parallel instance execution, project persistence, and auto-discovery capabilities. The implementation requires 8 new files spanning server-side logic (registry, spawner, port manager, discovery) and client-side UI components (launcher home, project cards, dialogs, hooks). The WebSocket protocol defines 7 message types for managing projects and instances."}],"totalDurationMs":39302,"totalTokens":15106,"totalToolUseCount":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":5,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2913,"cache_read_input_tokens":10578,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2913,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1610,"service_tier":"standard"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013TXrJkt1L15SQKMWfvCxez","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Summary of Exports and Functionality\n\nHere's a comprehensive breakdown of each file, their exports, and how they work together:\n\n---\n\n### 1. **projectRegistry.ts**\n**File Location:** `C:\\Users\\xsams\\RalphWiggumV3\\RalphWiggumV2\\dashboard\\server\\projectRegistry.ts`\n\n**Exports:**\n- `ProjectRegistry` (class)\n\n**Purpose:** Manages CRUD operations for registered projects and persists them to disk.\n\n**Key Methods:**\n- `init()` - Initializes the registry by loading from `~/.ralph/projects.json`\n- `listProjects()` - Returns all registered projects\n- `addProject(projectPath)` - Adds a new project, validates path exists, checks for Ralph-readiness\n- `removeProject(projectId)` - Removes a project by ID\n- `getProject(projectId)` - Retrieves a single project by ID\n- `updateLastOpened(projectId)` - Updates the lastOpened timestamp\n- `refreshRalphStatus(projectId)` - Re-checks if a project is Ralph-ready\n- `checkRalphReady(projectPath)` - Private method that checks for AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, or IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md\n- `fileExists(filePath)` - Private helper to check file existence\n\n**Data Storage:** Persists to `~/.ralph/projects.json` with structure:\n```typescript\n{\n  version: number,\n  projects: LauncherProject[]\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. **portManager.ts**\n**File Location:** `C:\\Users\\xsams\\RalphWiggumV3\\RalphWiggumV2\\dashboard\\server\\portManager.ts`\n\n**Exports:**\n- `PortManager` (class)\n- `PortPair` (interface)\n\n**Purpose:** Manages dynamic port allocation for dashboard instances, ensuring no port conflicts.\n\n**Key Methods:**\n- `allocate()` - Allocates next available backend/frontend port pair (starts at 3002/5174)\n- `release(backendPort, frontendPort)` - Releases ports when instance stops\n- `isPortAvailable(port)` - Checks if a specific port is available on the system\n- `getAllocatedCount()` - Returns count of currently allocated instances\n- `hasCapacity()` - Checks if more instances can be allocated (max 20)\n\n**Port Ranges:**\n- Backend: 3002-3021 (20 ports)\n- Frontend: 5174-5193 (20 ports)\n- Launcher uses: 3001/5173 (default dashboard ports)\n\n**PortPair Interface:**\n```typescript\n{\n  backendPort: number,\n  frontendPort: number\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. **instanceSpawner.ts**\n**File Location:** `C:\\Users\\xsams\\RalphWiggumV3\\RalphWiggumV2\\dashboard\\server\\instanceSpawner.ts`\n\n**Exports:**\n- `InstanceSpawner` (class, extends EventEmitter)\n\n**Purpose:** Spawns and manages dashboard backend instances, each running on dedicated ports for a specific project.\n\n**Key Methods:**\n- `spawnInstance(projectId, projectPath)` - Spawns a new dashboard instance\n- `stopInstance(projectId)` - Stops a running instance (handles Windows/Unix differently)\n- `listInstances()` - Returns all running instances\n- `isRunning(projectId)` - Checks if instance is running\n- `getInstance(projectId)` - Gets instance info for a project\n- `stopAll()` - Stops all running instances\n- `getDashboardUrl(projectId)` - Gets the dashboard URL for an instance\n\n**Events Emitted:**\n- `stopped` - When instance stops normally\n- `crashed` - When instance crashes or exits with error code\n\n**Internal Management:**\n- Uses `PortManager` for port allocation\n- Spawns Node.js child processes running the dashboard server\n- Handles process lifecycle (stdout, stderr, exit, error)\n- Platform-specific shutdown (taskkill on Windows, SIGTERM/SIGKILL on Unix)\n- Tracks instances with `projectId` as key\n\n**ManagedInstance (Internal):**\n```typescript\n{\n  projectId: string,\n  backendPort: number,\n  frontendPort: number,\n  pid: number,\n  startedAt: string,\n  process: ChildProcess,\n  portPair: PortPair\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. **projectDiscovery.ts**\n**File Location:** `C:\\Users\\xsams\\RalphWiggumV3\\RalphWiggumV2\\dashboard\\server\\projectDiscovery.ts`\n\n**Exports:**\n- `ProjectDiscovery` (class)\n\n**Purpose:** Scans directories to discover potential projects (git repos) that can be added to the launcher.\n\n**Key Methods:**\n- `updateRegistered(registeredProjects)` - Updates the list of already-registered projects\n- `discover()` - Scans common project directories (~/Projects, ~/Code, etc.)\n- `scanDirectory(dirPath)` - Scans a specific directory for projects\n- `scanCustomPath(customPath)` - Scans a user-provided custom path\n- `analyzeProject(projectPath)` - Private method that analyzes if a directory is a project\n- `checkRalphReady(projectPath)` - Private method checking for AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, or IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md\n- `hasFile(dir, filename)` - Private helper to check file existence\n- `hasDirectory(dir, subdir)` - Private helper to check subdirectory existence\n\n**Discovery Logic:**\n- Only considers directories with `.git` folder as projects\n- Checks for Ralph-readiness (AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md)\n- Scans common directories: Projects, Code, Development, dev, work, repos, GitHub, etc.\n- On Windows, also scans: C:\\Projects, C:\\Code, D:\\Projects, D:\\Code\n- Deduplicates discovered projects by path\n- Filters out: hidden dirs, node_modules, __pycache__, venv, .venv\n\n**DiscoveredProject (Return Type):**\n```typescript\n{\n  path: string,\n  name: string,\n  isGitRepo: boolean,\n  isRalphReady: boolean,\n  alreadyRegistered: boolean\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## How They Work Together\n\nThis is a **Project Launcher System** for the Ralph dashboard. Here's the data flow:\n\n### Registration Flow:\n1. **ProjectDiscovery** scans filesystem for git repositories\n2. User selects projects from discovered list\n3. **ProjectRegistry** adds them to `~/.ralph/projects.json` with Ralph-ready status\n\n### Launch Flow:\n1. User requests to launch a project from **ProjectRegistry**\n2. **PortManager** allocates available port pair (backend + frontend)\n3. **InstanceSpawner** spawns Node.js child process with:\n   - Allocated ports as environment variables\n   - Project path as PROJECT_PATH env var\n   - Dashboard server script as entry point\n4. Instance runs independently on its own ports\n5. **InstanceSpawner** monitors process lifecycle and emits events\n\n### Shutdown Flow:\n1. User requests to stop an instance\n2. **InstanceSpawner** kills the process (platform-specific)\n3. **PortManager** releases the ports back to the pool\n4. **ProjectRegistry** optionally updates lastOpened timestamp\n\n### Key Design Patterns:\n- **Singleton Registry**: Single source of truth for registered projects\n- **Resource Pooling**: PortManager maintains pool of 20 port pairs\n- **Process Isolation**: Each project runs in separate Node.js process\n- **Event-Driven**: InstanceSpawner uses EventEmitter for lifecycle events\n- **Platform Abstraction**: Handles Windows vs Unix differences for process management\n- **Discovery Pattern**: Non-intrusive scanning of common development directories\n\nThis architecture enables running multiple isolated dashboard instances simultaneously, each on different ports, managing different projects."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a5e4848 (for resuming to continue this agent's work if needed)"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2114ed30-b19e-4096-a327-936d9cd9d573","uuid":"877888b0-a04b-4093-a67a-ef8e2744e2c4","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Read these files and summarize their exports, functions, and how they work together:\n1. C:\\Users\\xsams\\RalphWiggumV3\\RalphWiggumV2\\dashboard\\server\\projectRegistry.ts\n2. C:\\Users\\xsams\\RalphWiggumV3\\RalphWiggumV2\\dashboard\\server\\portManager.ts\n3. C:\\Users\\xsams\\RalphWiggumV3\\RalphWiggumV2\\dashboard\\server\\instanceSpawner.ts\n4. C:\\Users\\xsams\\RalphWiggumV3\\RalphWiggumV2\\dashboard\\server\\projectDiscovery.ts\n\nFor each file, list all exported functions/classes/constants and describe what they do.","agentId":"a5e4848","content":[{"t